;;; A `(solve …)` claim splits a name that starts with `or` — **W22**, register
;;; row `W22/kw-or-in-solve` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`): a reserved word is a
;;; keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts, `(p a)` and `(orz a)`, and a `(solve …)` form claiming the
;;; state: `:expect (region (model (orz a) (p a)))`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `orz` is a name like any other. R is {∅},
;;; root holds both facts, and the claim holds: the answer of
;;; `kw-or-in-solve-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read the claim's `(orz a)` as the
;;; disjunction `(or z a)`, and the loader refused the program: every command
;;; exited 1 (`ein test`: 2) with *kb load error: (solve … :expect …) names or,
;;; which no declaration or fact makes a relation*. (`ein solve FILE` at
;;; `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same.) A
;;; keyword is a whole word now, at the boundary `grammar.md` § 1 states
;;; (Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation orz Node :why "orz holds of {?1}")
(p a) (orz a)
(solve :expect (region (model (orz a) (p a))))
